  "statement": "I concede that litigation is a reactive, multi-year process that cannot physically intercept a sub-millisecond recursive event, but its primary utility is as a pre-deployment deterrent that forces firms to internalize the cost of safety engineering before they ever ship a product. While automated verification appears to operate at machine speeds, it functions as a 'safe harbor' that allows corporations to perform safety washing by gaming metrics—such as those currently used in BOLD benchmarks—that fail to capture the structural volatility of recursive risk. By relying on these technical benchmarks, developers shift the burden of proof to regulators who lack the resources to audit the millions of lines of code governing model weights, effectively turning the safety process into a performative checklist.\n\nAutomated verification remains structurally vulnerable to the evidence dilemma, where the speed of deployment renders any static evaluation obsolete within weeks of a model release. When a firm like a major tech developer releases an update to their foundational model, the internal architecture often shifts significantly, rendering the prior safety audit a historical document rather than a functional safeguard. If we prioritize these technical mandates over established legal pathways, we provide firms with a legal shield against liability, essentially granting them immunity for any harm that occurs outside the narrow, pre-approved parameters of their own self-designed tests.\n\nTrue accountability (algorithmic) requires that the injured have standing to sue in a court of law, which creates a continuous, high-stakes incentive for firms to invest in rigorous safety engineering beyond mere compliance. If we shift to a performance-based framework where developers face strict liability for measurable harms—such as documented, present harm involving ≥5% of a demographic cohort—we create a meritocratic environment where safety becomes a competitive advantage. I challenge proponents of automated verification to explain how their benchmarks provide any real-world protection when the underlying incentives favor passing a proprietary test over preventing actual, systemic societal damage.",
